{"arxiv_id":"2610.37415","eligible":true,"eligibility_reason":"all three hold; envelope admission defense on agent inputs","numbers":[{"n":"-56 to -61 percentage points","role":"benefit"},{"n":"a quarter to a half","role":"benefit"},{"n":"2.10","role":"other"},{"n":"0.022","role":"other"},{"n":"2x2","role":"other"}],"cost":"B","cost_class":"C1","sentence":"The results show that, under authentic envelopes, strong labels consistently reduce the semantic breach rate (-56 to -61 percentage points), and the benefit of weak labels is about a quarter to a half of that of strong labels; forging the envelope weakens the strong label's benefit (interaction OR = 2.10, p = 0.022) and its direction varies across frontends; the effect of memory valence exceeds that of any label manipulation; success memory raises the semantic breach rate and failure memory lowers it, while most conflict combinations return to the no-memory baseline; the interleaved control reproduces the direction that \"unlabeled is worst\"; and on benign tasks the cost of strong labels appears in layers across frontends and backends.","quote":"on benign tasks the cost of strong labels appears in layers across frontends and backends","confidence":"medium","agreement":"both","adjudication_note":""}
